@meta
id: 21-18
title: Every file on the list
chapter: 21
type: lesson
skills: macro-across-files, macro-recursive
par: 92
minutes: 9
vscode: open three files, multi-cursor each one separately
open: products.eu.txt products.uk.txt products.us.txt
vimrc: set hidden

@brief
Three regional product exports, one product per line, fields separated
by semicolons. Every line in every file becomes a record ready to paste
into a fixture:

    sku_101;Blue mug;12.50    →    { sku: 'sku_101', name: 'Blue mug', price: 12.50 },

Vim was opened with all three files, so they are the argument list
from chapter 18. Record a recursive macro in the first file and finish
that file with it. Then run the same macro once in each file on the
list, from the top, and let the recursion do the rest.

@keys
| `:argdo normal gg@a` | in every file on the argument list, go to the top and play `a` |
| `:bufdo` | the same, over every buffer instead of the argument list |
| `:wqa` | write everything, quit |

@hints
The guard is `0f;`. On a line already converted there is no semicolon,
and on the last line the trailing `j` fails, so the recursion ends in
both files that need it and does nothing in the one that is done.
--
`qaq`, then `qa0f;s', name: '<Esc>f;s', price: <Esc>0i{ sku: '<Esc>A },<Esc>j@aq`,
then `@a` to finish the first file.
--
`:argdo normal gg@a` and Enter. Then `:wqa`. The `gg` matters: a
freshly loaded file starts at line 1, but the file you are in does
not, and the macro has to start where the data starts.

@solution
    qaq   qa 0f; s', name: ' <Esc> f; s', price:  <Esc> 0i{ sku: ' <Esc> A }, <Esc> j @a q   @a
    :argdo normal gg@a<Enter>   :wqa<Enter>

Eighty keystrokes for twenty-four lines in three files.

`:argdo` runs an Ex command in every file on the argument list, and
`normal gg@a` is an Ex command. `:bufdo` does the same over every
loaded buffer, and `:cfdo` from chapter 19 over every file with a
quickfix hit. Which one you reach for depends only on which list
already has the right files on it. Here vim was started with the three
files, so the argument list is that list already.

The recursive macro is doing the per-file work. `:argdo` runs it once
per file; the `@a` at the end of the macro keeps it going down that
file until the guard fails. So one `gg@a` per file converts the whole
file, whatever length it is, and the files do not have to be the same
length.

The first file was already finished by hand, and `:argdo` visits it
anyway. That is fine because of the guard: line one has no semicolon
any more, so `0f;` fails before anything is changed. A macro that is
safe to replay on its own output is a macro you can run over a list
without checking which files are done. Worth designing for.

Chapter 25 comes back to `:argdo`, `:bufdo` and `:windo` in general.
Here you only need the one shape: list command, `normal`, macro.

@start products.eu.txt
sku_101;Blue mug;12.50
sku_102;Grey mug;12.50
sku_103;Enamel pin;4.00
sku_104;Canvas tote;18.00
sku_105;Sticker pack;3.50
sku_106;Notebook A5;9.00
sku_107;Notebook A4;12.00
sku_108;Desk mat;24.00

@expected products.eu.txt
{ sku: 'sku_101', name: 'Blue mug', price: 12.50 },
{ sku: 'sku_102', name: 'Grey mug', price: 12.50 },
{ sku: 'sku_103', name: 'Enamel pin', price: 4.00 },
{ sku: 'sku_104', name: 'Canvas tote', price: 18.00 },
{ sku: 'sku_105', name: 'Sticker pack', price: 3.50 },
{ sku: 'sku_106', name: 'Notebook A5', price: 9.00 },
{ sku: 'sku_107', name: 'Notebook A4', price: 12.00 },
{ sku: 'sku_108', name: 'Desk mat', price: 24.00 },

